1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Every day, Frontier coding agents spend their tokens on one of the simplest and most universal failure modes.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Tool calls.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,500
I'm Deva, and this is my submission for the Meta Hackathon, Janus.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Now, here's a real, real agent working in a real repository.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,500
The command fails. It doesn't know why.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:21,500
The model can fix the error, but it costs time and resources.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:28,000
It takes around 800 reasoning tokens to diagnose why a tool call failed, and about 4 seconds on average.

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,000
Oh, facing the same failure with Janus alongside it, the agent just plays this through.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000
There's no Frontier call, no rejects, and you have diagnosis in milliseconds on GPU.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
From a 30-million parameter model that you can run on consumer hardware.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
So I ran 12 real failure modes through both setups.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,500
The lone model takes about 4 seconds of verdict and builds you about 800 reasoning tokens just to answer.

13
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:58,500
Janus answers in less than 10 milliseconds on a GPU and costs nothing.

14
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,500
It's the same question, and 400 times faster, and close to free.

15
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:06,500
So the trick is that Janus never generates text.

16
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:13,500
It takes one forward pass and gives you a probability over a set of typed decisions.

17
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:20,500
non-autoregressive encoder distilled from production agent traces with RLCD and the spirit of system-1 models.

18
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,500
A model that small has no business judging anything unless the question is very narrow.

19
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,500
This is a very real failure case, and this is a narrow question.

20
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:34,500
To wrap it up, Janus is a system-1 guard under a system-2 agent.

21
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:38,500
With it, the agent doesn't just drive tools anymore, but feels their failures.

22
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:43,500
Install it, go and break something, and watch your answer faster, right within Muse Code.

23
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:45,500
Thank you.

